  "worked_solution": "Let $m$ be the mass of $B.$\nThe length of the rod is $8\\,yd$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is $6\\,yd.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is\n\n$$\n8-6=2\\,yd,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117524/assets/f3efa18d3ae4e9a7f23f805dd314b9d04f50bb03ec3ead20a82a8e4c586e96a7.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-3\\cdot 6=-18\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $m\\cdot 2=2m\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18+2m & =0 \\\\ 2m & =18 \\\\ m & =9\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
